WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.960
Epigenetics is the study of changes&nbsp;
in gene function that are heritable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.160
and that are not attributed to&nbsp;
alterations of the DNA sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.240
Epigenetics can cause changes in gene expression&nbsp;
but that are not changes in the DNA sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.320
These mechanisms include DNA methylation,&nbsp;
histone modification, and non-coding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.840
RNA such as micro RNAs and the effect of&nbsp;
non-coding repeating regions in the DNA code.&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.200
Before we go into the specifics of&nbsp;
epigenetic variation or regulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.760
let's discuss the structure of DNA and&nbsp;
how it is organized in eukaryotic cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.320
As you remember, the foundational understanding&nbsp;
about phenotypes is that DNA codes for genes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.480
those genes are transcribed into mRNA which are&nbsp;
then translated into proteins. However, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.600
processes appear to be regulated by a variety of&nbsp;
mechanisms that are not directly recorded in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.760
actual code of DNA. In fact, individuals and&nbsp;
members of different species can have similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.760
DNA like twins or many conserved genes like humans&nbsp;
and monkeys, but significant phenotypic variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.160
can be easily observed that is the result of&nbsp;
epigenetic changes that are either established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.720
and passed through generations, or acquired&nbsp;
and lost throughout the organisms lifetime.&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.120
The eukaryotic cell has a nucleus that contains&nbsp;
tightly packaged DNA folded into structures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.120
we all know as chromosomes. These are packaged&nbsp;
together by the way of chromatin protein complexes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.440
that the DNA is wrapped around. On these images,&nbsp;
you can see how the DNA string has more bumps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:44.160
or beads. These visible beads are called histones.&nbsp;
When these beads were originally discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.720
scientists first confused them with genes until&nbsp;
eventually it was proven that the string-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.760
structures actually contained the genetic&nbsp;
code. Histones can be either grouped together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.880
forming lumps of DNA, or as we can see in the&nbsp;
picture on the right, relaxed and spread out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.560
The changes in the way DNA is packaged is used&nbsp;
for cell division when whole chromosomes need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.200
to be moved around or to regulate what code&nbsp;
is more or less accessible for transcription.&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:17.200
The regulation of DNA compactness is regulated&nbsp;
in several ways. One of these is DNA methylation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.240
Cytosine is one of the nucleotide that&nbsp;
can be modified by a methyl group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:29.680
CH3, catalyzed by a specialized enzyme called DNA&nbsp;
methyl transferase. In the first minutes of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.400
when we are composed of a single cell and this&nbsp;
epigenetic information has been wiped clean in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.760
the fertilized egg. The methyl groups have been&nbsp;
removed and every gene is like all the others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.680
Through the process of cell differentiation,&nbsp;
DNA methyl transferases add methyl groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.280
to genes shutting off some, in activating&nbsp;
others. When a cell divides, the epigenetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.320
information must be transmitted to each of the&nbsp;
new cells by a different DNA methyl transferase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:03.840
called DNMT1. DNMT1 adds the proper methyl&nbsp;
groups to these DNA strands as it is replicated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.000
The information is propagated in a tricky&nbsp;
way. The methyl groups almost always added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:18.160
to cytosine bases with these sequences, CG and&nbsp;
GC. Notice that both strands have a cytosine. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.840
in a methylated region of DNA, both strands will&nbsp;
have a methyl group when the DNA is replicated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.760
Each of the new DNA double helices will have&nbsp;
one old strand, complete with methyl groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:36.800
and one new strand which is not methylated. So&nbsp;
DNMT1 just needs to look for the CG bases sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.080
where only one strand has a methyl group.
The basic repeating unit of chromatin is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.440
the nucleosome, in which hundred and forty-six&nbsp;
base pairs of DNA wraps around an octamer of core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:55.440
histones consisting of pairs of H3, H4, H2A, and&nbsp;
H2B. The terminal tails of histones protrude out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.800
of the nucleosome and are subject to a variety&nbsp;
of post translational modifications, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.680
acetylation, phosphorylation, ubiquitination,&nbsp;
and lysine and arginine methylation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.120
Acetylation was the first of these modifications&nbsp;
to be linked with active transcription.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.280
Subsequently, phosphorylation of histone&nbsp;
H3 was found to cooperate with a situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.800
in translational activation. Some histone&nbsp;
methylation events have also been associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.120
with transcription activation, and others with&nbsp;
gene silencing. One important aspect of histones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.240
is that they can be changed to alter how&nbsp;
much packing the DNA is capable of. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:40.800
several modifications that affect how well DNA is&nbsp;
packaged. The basic regulation is done via groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:46.720
of atoms that are at the ends of histones. These&nbsp;
can be of several types and will have a positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.560
or a negative charge that either attract them&nbsp;
together or force them apart. The DNA region that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.440
is wrapped around the histones can be more or less&nbsp;
accessible causing variation in gene expression.&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.760
Histone proteins or nucleosomes undergo a host&nbsp;
of different post translational modifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.040
including phosphorylation, acetylation, and&nbsp;
methylation, which have profound effects on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:15.280
remodeling of chromatin. Histone modifications can&nbsp;
function either individually or combinatorially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:21.760
to govern such processes as transcription,&nbsp;
replication, DNA repair and apoptosis. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:28.880
methyl groups of atoms increased packing. Acetyl&nbsp;
group decreases packing. Phosphoryl group can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.400
attached to the histones and cause a decrease&nbsp;
in packing. These groups of atoms are attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:40.800
to various types of histones at various locations.
As the DNA is wrapped around the histones and can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.960
be more or less accessible, various&nbsp;
positions of the DNA are affected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:52.320
These include the promoter region, transcription&nbsp;
start sites, and the introns and exons of genes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.720
Each one of these elements can have a significant&nbsp;
effect on the level of gene expression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:05.840
on alternative splicing, as well as&nbsp;
other downstream regulation of pathways.

